Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Stair Keeps the Missing Kick
- Story & Context: During the first slope set, Joana places the portable drum speaker at the bottom of the entrance stair while the acoustic players remain above. One kick disappears from every second two-bar cycle because the speaker briefly overloads on the steep concrete landing. Instead of fixing the fault, the band turns it into structure. Mila plays a three-note gadulka phrase that lands exactly where the missing kick should have been, while Lidia answers with a deep floor-tom stroke one eighth-note later.

Caio stays silent for the first four cycles, then enters with one euphonium pedal during the longest electronic bass gap. The central section increases snare density but preserves the missing-kick rule. By the final minute, dancers anticipate the absence strongly enough that the missing low hit feels like a physical step in the groove.

The track ends with the electronic speaker muted completely while gadulka, tom, and euphonium maintain the pattern acoustically for eight bars.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A steep concrete stair inside a civic gym at night, portable speaker glowing at the bottom landing, gadulka player above, deep floor tom halfway up, euphonium bell reflecting streetlight. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “leave the kick,” “take the stair,” “eight more,” and “hold the floor.”

Track 2: Rosin Dust on the Free-Throw Line
- Story & Context: Mila notices pale bow rosin collecting across one painted free-throw line after rehearsal. Joana uses the line as a formal boundary: electronic bass can only enter after Mila completes a phrase while standing on one side of the mark. The gadulka part becomes more rhythmically aggressive, full of quick slides, repeated bowed attacks, and clipped open-string drones.

Lidia plays a dry palm pattern on hand drum with one resonant floor-tom note at the end of every fourth bar. Caio answers those resonant notes with short falling brass intervals rather than pedals. Midway through, Mila steps across the line early, but Joana deliberately withholds the sub for another two bars. The delay creates a stronger sense of pull than the expected bass entrance would have.

The final section removes all clap layers and leaves only kick, gadulka, tom, and one breathy euphonium tone.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: White free-throw line across scratched gym floor dusted with pale rosin, gadulka bow hair and resin particles visible in close focus, floor tom and euphonium nearby, compact drum controller on a folding chair. Spoken fragments limited to “cross the line,” “not yet,” “two bars later,” and “leave the clap out.”

Track 3: Bus Light Through the Open Gym Door
- Story & Context: The EP closer begins with the gym door fully open to the steep street below. Passing buses throw moving bands of light across the court every few minutes. Joana maps no samples from the street; the outside world remains purely environmental. Mila holds longer gadulka drones than elsewhere on the EP, allowing sympathetic strings to ring into the room while the brega funk pattern stays unusually sparse.

Caio uses soft euphonium fifths only when a bus passes the doorway. Lidia answers with tom rolls that stop before the vehicle light disappears. Halfway through the piece, Joana cuts the electronic bass entirely and leaves only short kick/snare fragments beneath the acoustic ensemble.

In the final thirty-two bars, the programmed drums gradually thin until only one clap remains every two bars. The last event is not electronic: a bus light crosses the open door while Mila releases one low bowed note and the room microphones capture the fade of the floor tom.
